039794921875, -0.01593017578125, 0.022369384765625, 0.0011472702026367188, -0.0005140304565429688, 0.0460205078125, -0.0199737548828125, 0.01534271240234375, -0.01055908203125, 0.01503753662109375, 0.0158538818359375, 0.0234832763671875, 0.03680419921875, -0.0022144317626953125, 0.0462646484375, 0.0146026611328125, 0.036651611328125, -0.01131439208984375, -0.0008511543273925781, -0.01213836669921875, -0.0241851806640625, 0.003551483154296875, -0.00787353515625, -0.0200347900390625, 0.0111236572265625, 0.044036865234375, -0.00911712646484375, -0.0161285400390625, -0.0294952392578125, 0.0237884521484375, -0.0030536651611328125, 0.00672149658203125, 0.05078125, 0.015869140625, -0.0151519775390625, -0.0301513671875, -0.0014829635620117188, 0.00345611572265625, 0.001110076904296875, -0.0296630859375, 0.0147552490234375, -0.01021575927734375, -0.0009112358093261719, -0.00690460205078125, -0.01155853271484375, 0.020599365234375, 0.0162506103515625, -0.0044403076171875, -0.010650634765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\\section*{Introduction}\n\nLarge-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines typically optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, limiting the synergy between different generative tasks. A central challenge is not only how to curate each task-specific corpus, but also how to organize heterogeneous supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\n\\section*{Methodology}\n\nWe introduce a \\textbf{capability-driven data infrastructure}, a framework that systematically organizes and curates data to support the progressive acquisition of generative capabilities. Here, \"capability-driven\" refers to structuring data and training processes around the specific skills or functionalities a generative model must acquire, such as text-to-image synthesis, image editing, or knowledge grounding. This infrastructure integrates \\textbf{capability-aligned curriculum scheduling}, which sequences data exposure based on the dependencies among tasks\u2014meaning that simpler or foundational tasks are introduced before more complex ones, facilitating more effective model training.\n\nThe framework consists of three specialized yet interoperable data engines:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Text-Image Grounding Engine}: Constructs supervision for aligning textual descriptions with images, enabling robust text-to-image (T2I) generation. For example, it pairs captions with corresponding images to teach the model basic T2I mapping.\n    \\item \\textbf{Inter-Image Transformation Engine}: Builds paired data for image editing and transformation tasks, supporting capabilities such as inpainting, style transfer, and object manipulation. This engine, for instance, generates before-and-after image pairs for editing tasks.\n    \\item \\textbf{Image-Knowledge Association Engine}: Associates images with structured knowledge entities, enhancing the model's ability to ground visual content in external knowledge, such as linking an image of a landmark to its Wikipedia entry.\n\\end{itemize}\nCaption experts further align T2I and editing supervision across tasks and granularities, ensuring consistency and interoperability.\n\nA multi-stage curriculum jointly evolves task composition, visual-concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution along the dependency order of capability acquisition. This curriculum is guided by \\textbf{capability-aware evaluation}, which employs targeted retrieval (measuring the model's ability to retrieve relevant images given a query), expert construction (manual or semi-automated creation of challenging evaluation sets), and gap-aware resampling (identifying and supplementing underrepresented concepts or tasks in the data) to iteratively refine the data. Evaluation metrics include retrieval accuracy (the percentage of correct image retrievals given a text or concept query), coverage of visual concepts (the proportion of unique concepts represented in the dataset), and performance on downstream generative tasks (such as FID or human evaluation scores for generated images).\n\nIn summary, this methodology enables the systematic construction and evaluation of datasets that support the progressive development of multiple generative capabilities within a unified framework, setting the stage for the results that follow.\n\n\\section*{Results}\n\nAt scale, our infrastructure curates a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs. These numbers represent a significant increase over existing datasets, enabling broader coverage of visual concepts and more diverse generative capabilities. For comparison, previous large-scale datasets such as LAION-400M focus primarily on T2I pairs, while our framework extends to editing and knowledge association at unprecedented scale.\n\nWe train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at two scales, with 3B and 6B parameters, respectively. Quantitative evaluation on CPI-Bench demonstrates improvements in visual coverage and generative versatility. Here, \\textit{visual coverage} refers to the diversity and breadth of visual concepts the model can represent, measured by the number of unique concepts accurately generated or retrieved. \\textit{Generative versatility} denotes the model's ability to perform a range of tasks (e.g., T2I, editing, knowledge grounding) with high fidelity, assessed via task-specific metrics such as retrieval accuracy and concept diversity. For example, our models achieve a 7\\% higher retrieval accuracy (top-1 accuracy on CPI-Bench) and a 12\\% increase in concept diversity (number of unique concepts generated) compared to baselines. Table~1 provides a comparative summary of key metrics across our models and LAION-400M-based baselines.\n\nQualitative evaluations across diverse text-to-image and editing scenarios further illustrate effective transfer and rendering fidelity. Figure~1 shows representative outputs: (a) text-to-image generations with rare concepts, (b) image editing results (e.g., object insertion/removal), and (c) knowledge-grounded image generation, highlighting the model's versatility and fidelity.\n\n\\section*{Conclusion}\n\nOur capability-driven data infrastructure enables systematic curation and organization of large-scale, heterogeneous supervision for image generation. By aligning data construction and curriculum scheduling with generative capability dependencies, we achieve broad visual coverage, versatile rendering, and effective transfer across tasks. While our framework demonstrates significant improvements in data scale and model performance, potential limitations include the computational cost of large-scale data curation, challenges in maintaining annotation quality at scale, and the need for further research on curriculum optimization for emerging tasks. Nonetheless, this framework advances the state of the art in multimodal generative modeling and provides a scalable foundation for future research."}}